    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d252-a1">

                <pb facs="0523" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1864p2/pg503"/>

                <head>Mr. Archibald to Lord
                        Lyons.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">British Consulate, New
                            York</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-01-21"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >January</hi> 21, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Lord</hi>: I have the honor to call your
                    lordship’s attention to the enclosed ro promulgated by Major General Dix,
                    commanding the department of the east, in reference to the commission appointed
                    to investigate the cases of persons crested and imprisoned at Fort Lafayette and
                    Fort Warren. The order was published in the newspapers of yesterday, and this
                    morning I called at General Dix’s headquarters to ascertain the mode in which
                    the proposed <pb facs="0524" n="504" xml:id="pg504"/> investigations are to be
                    conducted in reference to the cases of British subjects imprisoned at Fort
                    Lafayette. I was referred to Major Bolles, the judge advocate, who, with two
                    other members of the commission, was then at the headquarters, and was informed
                    by him that there would be, in the first instance, an examination of an <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ex parte</hi> character, in the nature of an inquiry by
                    a grand jury, after which, if a further investigation were deemed necessary, the
                    parties would be duly notified, and, as I am led to infer, counsel would be
                    permitted to assist them in their defence of any charges which might be brought
                    against them. In any case in which a further investigation might be deemed
                    unnecessary, the commission would recommend the discharge of the arrested
                    party.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>N. M. ARCHIBALD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lord Lyons.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
